{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-96cf6b2a186154b3", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t                                                  AeroReformer2: Spoken-Query Referring Segmentation for Aerial Images\n2\t\n3\t                                                                                          Rui Lia , Chenxi Duanb , Haoyang Yangc,∗\n4\t                                                    a Institute for Data, Systems, and Society, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts 02139, United States\n5\t                                                 b Department of Civil Engineering and Management, School of Engineering, The University of Manchester, Manchester, United Kingdom\n6\t                                            c Department of Aeronautical and Aviation Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, China\n7\t\n8\t\n9\t\n10\t\n11\t                              Abstract\n12\t                              Spoken language offers a natural, hands-free interface for specifying an arbitrary target in dense remote-sensing imagery, yet\n13\t                              existing referring remote-sensing image segmentation benchmarks accept only written expressions. To bridge this gap, we introduce\n14\t                              VoiceAeroRef, a spoken-query benchmark derived from RISBench that adds accent- and voice-diverse speech while preserving the\n15\t                              original image, mask, and data splits. Its hard evaluation set combines rotor, wind, and mixed interference with three signal-to-noise\n16\t                              levels. We also propose AeroReformer2, an efficient bilateral network that combines a boundary-preserving visual path with token-\n17\t                              preserving speech encoding, kernel linear cross-modal attention, and a resolution refinement head. The design conditions visual\n18\t                              features at two scales without materializing a dense speech–visual affinity matrix, then restores fine boundaries using high-resolution\n19\tarXiv:2608.08874v1 [cs.CV] 9 Aug 2026\n20\t\n21\t\n22\t\n23\t\n24\t                              visual features. On the clean test split, AeroReformer2 with Swin-Base achieves 62.09% mean intersection over union (mIoU) and\n25\t                              68.22% overall intersection over union (oIoU), outperforming the strongest audio-adapted remote-sensing baseline by 5.38 and 2.08\n26\t                              percentage points, respectively. It retains the best hard-set mIoU at 54.09%. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first benchmark\n27\t                              and model study of full-sentence spoken-query referring segmentation for remote-sensing imagery. The code will be made publicly\n28\t                              available at https://github.com/lironui/AeroReformer2.\n29\t                              Keywords: Multimodal, Transformer, Spoken-Query Referring Segmentation, Deep Learning, Remote Sensing\n30\t\n31\t\n32\t                               1. Introduction                                                                        many small, adjacent instances. A fixed command menu avoids\n33\t                                                                                                                      ambiguity only by restricting what the user can request. In con-\n34\t                                 High-resolution aerial and satellite remote sensing supports in-                     trast, direct spoken language is a natural interaction modality for\n35\t                              frastructure mapping, emergency response, precision agriculture,                        an analyst interpreting remote-sensing imagery: people already\n36\t                              traffic analysis, environmental monitoring, and large-area Earth     