afde017ad2de", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T00:49:24.147Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.08874v1.txt", "content": "                                                  AeroReformer2: Spoken-Query Referring Segmentation for Aerial Images\n\n                                                                                          Rui Lia , Chenxi Duanb , Haoyang Yangc,∗\n                                                    a Institute for Data, Systems, and Society, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts 02139, United States\n                                                 b Department of Civil Engineering and Management, School of Engineering, The University of Manchester, Manchester, United Kingdom\n                                            c Department of Aeronautical and Aviation Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, China\n\n\n\n\n                              Abstract\n                              Spoken language offers a natural, hands-free interface for specifying an arbitrary target in dense remote-sensing imagery, yet\n                              existing referring remote-sensing image segmentation benchmarks accept only written expressions. To bridge this gap, we introduce\n                              VoiceAeroRef, a spoken-query benchmark derived from RISBench that adds accent- and voice-diverse speech while preserving the\n                              original image, mask, and data splits. Its hard evaluation set combines rotor, wind, and mixed interference with three signal-to-noise\n                              levels. We also propose AeroReformer2, an efficient bilateral network that combines a boundary-preserving visual path with token-\n                              preserving speech encoding, kernel linear cross-modal attention, and a resolution refinement head. The design conditions visual\n                              features at two scales without materializing a dense speech–visual affinity matrix, then restores fine boundaries using high-resolution\narXiv:2608.08874v1 [cs.CV] 9 Aug 2026\n\n\n\n\n                              visual features. On the clean test split, AeroReformer2 with Swin-Base achieves 62.09% mean intersection over union (mIoU) and\n                              68.22% overall intersection over union (oIoU), outperforming the strongest audio-adapted remote-sensing baseline by 5.38 and 2.08\n                              percentage points, respectively. It retains the best hard-set mIoU at 54.09%. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first benchmark\n                              and model study of full-sentence spoken-query referring segmentation for remote-sensing imagery. The code will be made publicly\n                              available at https://github.com/lironui/AeroReformer2.\n                              Keywords: Multimodal, Transformer, Spoken-Query Referring Segmentation, Deep Learning, Remote Sensing\n\n\n                               1. Introduction                                                                        many small, adjacent instances. A fixed command menu avoids\n                                                                                                                      ambiguity only by restricting what the user can request. In con-\n                                 High-resolution aerial and satellite remote sensing supports in-                     trast, direct spoken language is a natural interaction modality for\n                              frastructure mapping, emergency response, precision agriculture,                        an analyst interpreting remote-sensing imagery: people already\n                              traffic analysis, environmental monitoring, and large-area Earth                        describe targets through category words, attributes, landmarks,\n                              observation [1, 2, 3]. Across these applications, pixel-level per-                      and spatial relations, and an utterance can express all of these\n                              ception is indispensable: an image-analysis system must not only                        cues in one command while leaving the display available for\n                              recognize that roads, buildings, vehicles, vessels, vegetation, or                      image inspection. Speech therefore provides a direct bridge\n                              damaged regions exist, but also delineate their precise spatial                         between human intent and pixel-level remote-sensing analysis\n                              extent. Modern semantic segmentation has made this form of                              rather than merely replacing one graphical pointing device with\n                              scene understanding increasingly reliable [2, 4, 5, 6, 7]. Yet                          another.\n                              semantic segmentation presumes a fixed class vocabulary and\n                              produces every supported category regardless of the analyst’s                              Referring image segmentation (RIS) provides the dense-\n                              immediate intent. This is poorly matched to an interactive work-                        prediction formulation needed for that bridge. Given an im-\n                              flow in which a person may care about one particular object—for                         age and a free-form expression, RIS predicts exactly the pix-\n                              example, “the small vehicle immediately below the bridge,” “the                         els belonging to the described referent [17, 18, 19]. Unlike\n                              vessel at the right-most pier,” or “the irregular pool nearest the                      semantic segmentation, the task can distinguish multiple ob-\n                      